To call a tool, output a <tool_call> block anywhere in your response:
<tool_call>{"name": "<tool_name>", "arguments": {"param": "value", ...}}</tool_call>
You may call multiple tools per response. Available tools:
  exec(command) — Execute a shell command on this node and return its output. Use this for any network inspection or configuration: 'ip addr show', 'ip route show', 'ip route add ...', 'ip route del ...', 'ping -c 3 <ip>', 'ip link show', 'ip neigh show', etc. 
    - command: The shell command to run, e.g. 'ip route show'
  report_done(message, success) — Signal that you want to terminate and are satisfied with the current state of the entire network.
    - message: Summary of what was configured and connectivity verified
    - success: True if full connectivity was achieved
  send_message(to, message) — Send a natural language message to another node's agent. The message will be injected into the recipient's context on its next iteration.
    - to: Name of the destination node, e.g. 'h2'
    - message: The message to send
  wait([timeout]) — Block until a message arrives from another node (or until timeout). Use this when you have completed your local actions and need to wait for other nodes to respond or coordinate before proceeding.
    - timeout (optional): Maximum seconds to wait for a message (default: 5)
  get_traffic_sample() — Take a live measurement of the current per-provider throughput.
